  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\n\nMid level ridging currently over the region will get pushed to the ne\ntonight ahead of a strengthening upper low. As the upper low takes\nhold, cold air will get wrapped in and then be followed by strong nly\nflow Fri and back to wly on Sat. A cooling airmass will be found\nthrough Friday, then begin to warm to at or slightly above normal for\nthe weekend and into next week.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2)\n\nA strengthening cutoff low is expected to undercut the Ohio Valley,\nmoving from the Midwest Wednesday evening and overnight, to the\nTennessee Valley on Thanksgiving, and then pivot ne into WV/PA\novernight. This is per the GFS, where the Euro and Canadian track the\ncenter of the low through the CWA Thurs evening/overnight before\nmoving e into PA. \n\nIn any of these scenarios, rain will overspread the region early on \nThanksgiving, covering northern Kentucky before daybreak and \nspreading northward to reach the I-70 corridor shortly thereafter. \nSome breaks are expected in sern CWA but will be few and far in- \nbetween with more rounds of rain lasting through the evening. This \nlooks to be a long, soaking rainfall for the region with 1 to 1 1/4\" \nexpected. \n\nMuch cooler air will also bring dryness and cut the rainfall off\ntowards Friday morning. As the rain ends with the cold air \nintrusion during the day, some snow may mix in before it tapers off \nentirely. Not looking for any accumulations, and if it did come to \npass, would only be found on grassy/elevated surfaces. Will continue \nto watch later runs and pcpn rates as this can overcome warm ground \ntemperatures and lead to minor impacts as was observed last November.",
